  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Verify that differentiation-based continuous-time input-output rules are linear by applying additivity and homogeneity through the superposition test.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Linear Derivative Systems",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A first-derivative system takes an input signal $x(t)$ and produces the output $y(t)=[x(t)]'$. The linearity test for this kind of system is whether the derivative rule preserves both sums and scalar multiples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=[x(t)]'",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For additivity, the derivative must distribute across an input sum. If the system is linear, then the derivative of $x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ must equal the sum of the two separate derivatives.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x_1+x_2)'(t)=x_1'(t)+x_2'(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For homogeneity, a constant factor must pass through the derivative unchanged. If the input is scaled by $a$, then the output must scale by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(a x(t))'=a x'(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Those two identities are the superposition test for a derivative system. A derivative rule is linear only when the response to a scaled sum matches the scaled sum of the separate responses, so later examples will check both identities in symbolic form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left(a_1 x_1 + a_2 x_2\\right)' = a_1 x_1' + a_2 x_2'",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Testing Additivity for a First-Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x_1(t)=t^2$ and $x_2(t)=3\\cos t$, test additivity for $y(t)=[x(t)]'$ by comparing the derivative of $x_1+x_2$ with $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a summed input, so we check whether the derivative of the sum matches the sum of the separate derivatives. For this system, the output rule is $y[x](t)=x'(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)=t^2+3\\cos t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Differentiate the combined input directly, then differentiate each input separately. If additivity holds, both paths should produce the same output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\ny[x](t) &= x'(t) = (t^2+3\\cos t)' = 2t-3\\sin t \\\\\ny[x_1](t) &= (t^2)' = 2t \\\\\ny[x_2](t) &= (3\\cos t)' = -3\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compare the two results: $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2t-3\\sin t$, which matches $y[x](t)$. So this first-derivative system passes the additivity test for these inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing Homogeneity for a First-Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=t^3-2t$ and $a=4$, test homogeneity by comparing $y[a x](t)$ with $a y[x](t)$ for $y(t)=[x(t)]'$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a scaled input $a x(t)$, so we check whether the derivative keeps that same scale outside the output. For this system, $y[x](t)=x'(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=t^3-2t,\\qquad a=4,\\qquad a x(t)=4(t^3-2t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Differentiate the scaled input directly, then differentiate first and scale the output. If homogeneity holds, both paths should give the same expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\ny[a x](t) &= \\big(4(t^3-2t)\\big)' = 12t^2-8 \\\\\ny[x](t) &= (t^3-2t)' = 3t^2-2 \\\\\na y[x](t) &= 4(3t^2-2) = 12t^2-8\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $y[a x](t)=a y[x](t)$, this first-derivative system passes the homogeneity test for this input and scale. That is the scalar-multiplication half of linearity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying Superposition to a Scaled Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=[x(t)]'$, $a=2$, $x_1(t)=t^3$, and $x_2(t)=\\sin t$, compute $y[a x_1+x_2]$ and compare with $a y[x_1]+y[x_2]$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a scaled sum of two inputs, so we check whether the derivative of the combined input matches the scaled sum of the separate outputs. For this system, $y[x](t)=x'(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t)=a x_1(t)+x_2(t)=2t^3+\\sin t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Differentiate the combined input directly, then differentiate each input separately and scale only the $x_1$ output by $a$. If superposition holds, the two results will match term by term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\ny[u](t) &= \\big(2t^3+\\sin t\\big)' = 6t^2+\\cos t \\\\\ny[x_1](t) &= (t^3)' = 3t^2 \\\\\ny[x_2](t) &= (\\sin t)' = \\cos t \\\\\na y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t) &= 2(3t^2)+\\cos t = 6t^2+\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $y[u](t)=a y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)$, the first-derivative system passes the superposition test for this scaled-sum input. This combines additivity and homogeneity in one linearity check.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Testing a System with an Input Term and a Derivative Term",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test system $y(t)=x(t)+x'(t)$ on $x_1(t)=e^t$ and $x_2(t)=t^2$ by evaluating $y[x_1+x_2]$ and $y[x_1]+y[x_2]$ and then with scale $a=-2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the mixed rule $y[x]=x+x'$. It adds the input itself and its first derivative, so the linearity check is to compare the response to $x_1+x_2$ with the sum of the separate responses, then check one scalar scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y[x_1+x_2] = (x_1+x_2) + (x_1+x_2)' = (e^t+t^2) + (e^t+2t) = 2e^t+t^2+2t\n\\[6pt]\ny[x_1] + y[x_2] = (x_1+x_1') + (x_2+x_2') = (e^t+e^t) + (t^2+2t) = 2e^t+t^2+2t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions match, so additivity holds for this mixed operator. The same structure should also preserve scaling, so we test one negative scalar next.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y[-2(x_1+x_2)] = -2(x_1+x_2) + \\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl[-2(x_1+x_2)\\bigr] = -2(e^t+t^2) - 2(e^t+2t) = -4e^t-2t^2-4t\n\\[6pt]\n-2\\,y[x_1+x_2] = -2\\bigl((e^t+t^2) + (e^t+2t)\\bigr) = -4e^t-2t^2-4t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the scaled output matches the output scaled by $-2$, this system passes both parts of the superposition test. The mixed input-plus-derivative rule is linear because each branch is linear and they combine without any constant offset.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Verify that differentiation-based continuous-time input-output rules are linear by applying additivity and homogeneity through the superposition test.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Testing Additivity for a First-Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=x'(t)$, let $x_1(t)=t^3$ and $x_2(t)=4\\sin t$. What is the correct result of the additivity check for $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3t^2 \\cdot 4\\cos t$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3t^2+4\\sin t$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2+4\\sin t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2-4\\cos t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2+4$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "$x(t)=t^3+4\\sin t$, so $y[x](t)=\\dfrac{d}{dt}(t^3+4\\sin t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$. Also $y[x_1](t)=3t^2$ and $y[x_2](t)=4\\cos t$, so $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=3t^2+4\\cos t$. The expressions match.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=x'(t)$, test additivity with $x_1(t)=2t$ and $x_2(t)=e^{t}$. Which option is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=2+e^{t}$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+e^{t}$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=2e^{t}$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+e^{t}$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=2+e^{t}$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+2e^{t}$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=2+e^{2t}$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+e^{t}$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[x](t)=3+e^{t}$ and $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+e^{t}$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $x(t)=2t+e^t$, differentiate: $y[x](t)=\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2t+e^t)=2+e^t$. Separately, $y[x_1](t)=\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2t)=2$ and $y[x_2](t)=\\dfrac{d}{dt}(e^t)=e^t$, so $y[x_1](t)+y[x_2](t)=2+e^t$. The summed-input output equals the sum of outputs.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Linear Derivative Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing Homogeneity for a First-Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=x'(t)$, $x(t)=t^2+3t$, and $a=-2$, compare $y[-2x](t)$ with $-2y[x](t)$. Which option is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[-2x](t)=(-2x(t))'=-4t-6$ and $-2y[x](t)=-2(2t+3)=-4t-6$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[-2x](t)=(-2x(t))'=-2t-3$ and $-2y[x](t)=-4t-6$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[-2x](t)=(-2t^2-6t)'=-4t-6$ and $-2y[x](t)=-(2t+3)/2$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[-2x](t)=(-2x(t))'=-4t-6$ and $-2y[x](t)=4t+6$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y[-2x](t)=(-2t^2+6t)'=-4t+6$ and $-2y[x](t)=-4t-6$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
